from pathlib import Path
import re,html
p=Path(__file__).resolve().parents[1]/'dist';e=html.escape
# Editorial expansions grounded in the existing project descriptions and linked papers.
D={
'Resource-aware adversarial resilience':('Attacks can change more than a prediction: they can also increase the time and energy required to process sensor data. This project examines that broader attack surface and the resource costs introduced by defenses.','Evaluate predictive performance alongside energy and latency, including the tradeoffs created by model pruning.','The published Sponge Attacks study provides a sensing-AI foundation. Comparisons should account for model, device and workload so that apparent efficiency gains are not confused with reduced utility.','Resource-constrained sensing and edge platforms need dependable response times as well as accurate outputs. These evaluations help identify when a defense shifts costs elsewhere rather than removing them.',['Sensing workload','Attack / defense','Accuracy + energy + latency']),
'Sustainable machine learning':('Security measures are part of the computational workload of AI. Training, repeated evaluation and adversarial defenses can change energy requirements and their associated environmental costs.','Make resource and environmental measurements transparent and compare the costs of learning and defense under clearly stated assumptions.','Published work reviews carbon-emission quantification and examines the sustainability and social costs of adversarial training. The approach emphasizes the measurement boundary, hardware and energy assumptions behind an estimate.','Researchers can use these distinctions to report security–efficiency tradeoffs more responsibly. Estimated carbon costs are not equivalent to measured reductions in operational emissions.',['Training & defense','Measure resource use','Explain tradeoffs']),
'AI infrastructure and agent systems':('As AI workloads move between devices, edge services and larger infrastructure, security and resource decisions become interconnected. Agent workflows introduce additional questions about the information and computation passed between components.','Develop an evaluation agenda for trustworthy coordination, resource allocation and evidence flow across connected AI systems.','This is a proposed systems direction informed by existing efficiency and security research. Data-center-scale evaluation is a future target; no dedicated infrastructure deployment or project-specific performance result is asserted.','The long-term relevance is to resource-aware AI services whose security cannot be assessed at the model level alone. Operational impact will require system-scale experiments and independent validation.',['Devices & agents','Edge / cloud coordination','Security + resource budgets']),
'Wearable AI across the lifecycle':('Wearable learning pipelines turn time-varying measurements into activity and wellbeing inferences. Changes to training signals, labels or cross-modal alignment can affect both predictive behavior and the trustworthiness of subsequent explanations.','Study data integrity, poisoning detection and sanitization while evaluating whether useful activity information is retained.','Existing studies examine wearable activity recognition, poisoning and privacy-aware distributed learning. The LLM-based detection work is a preprint; detection, localization and recovery must be evaluated separately rather than treated as interchangeable successes.','The work supports research on dependable sensing pipelines in healthcare, wellbeing and connected environments. Benchmark performance does not by itself establish clinical effectiveness or safety.',['Sensor data','Integrity checks','Reliable interpretation']),
'Privacy in human–AI interaction':('Everyday writing and audio-editing tools may reveal sensitive attributes, but may also help transform content to reduce unwanted inference. The same consumer interface can therefore act as both a privacy risk and a possible defense.','Measure how transformations affect sensitive-attribute inference while preserving the usefulness and meaning of the original content.','Published writing-assistant and audio-editing studies motivate controlled comparisons across original and transformed inputs. Privacy protection, task utility and model-specific behavior must be assessed together; fluent rewrites alone do not demonstrate protection.','Relevant settings include consumer platforms, communication tools and device-integrated assistants using LLMs. Results support informed evaluation of privacy tradeoffs, not universal guarantees across people, models or platforms.',['Text / audio','Consumer AI transformation','Privacy ↔ utility']),
'Language models for cyber-physical systems':('Language models can connect sensor observations with human-readable descriptions and higher-level decisions. Physical environments also impose constraints that text-only benchmarks may miss, including timing, incomplete measurements and consequences of erroneous outputs.','Explore useful language-assisted sensing and interpretation while preserving privacy, reliability and resource awareness.','Published architectures and fall-detection studies provide foundations, alongside wearable-data integrity research. The evaluation agenda connects sensor evidence to model output and considers uncertainty and human oversight rather than assuming autonomous control is safe.','Applications include smart environments, wearable assistance and healthcare-oriented research prototypes. Each setting requires separate testing of detection errors, response behavior and user impact.',['Physical observations','Language-assisted interpretation','Human oversight']),
'On-device generative image-edit forensics':('Consumer editing tools can alter a localized part of an image while leaving most of the scene intact. Detecting these changes requires attention to real device workflows, not only to images generated entirely from scratch.','Detect and localize consumer-tool edits, evaluate multilingual edit descriptions and examine the effects of compression.','The published Edits in the Wild work introduces ConsumerGenEdit as a benchmark grounded in consumer platforms. Its intellectual contribution is an evaluation setting that links localized edits, platform conditions and language-based descriptions.','The benchmark is relevant to provenance research and potential fact-checking workflows. An edit localization is evidence about a modification, not proof of malicious intent or a complete history of the image.',['Consumer image edit','Detect & localize','Provenance evidence']),
'AI application forensics':('AI applications leave digital traces beyond their visible conversations. Understanding which artifacts persist, and what they can legitimately establish, is important for reproducible examination of emerging interfaces.','Identify and characterize traces associated with the ChatGPT Windows application and their interpretation in forensic research.','The linked preprint studies application artifacts. A sound approach distinguishes an observed trace from an inferred user action, records application conditions, and considers how versions or settings may change artifact availability.','Potential use lies in authorized forensic examination and research. Any operational use requires lawful access, independent validation and appropriate evidence-handling procedures.',['Application activity','Recoverable artifacts','Qualified interpretation']),
'Localized quantum learning with Q-Capsule':('Training quantum neural models can become difficult when optimization landscapes yield very small gradients. Q-Capsule explores a localized capsule-based architecture as a way to investigate this trainability problem.','Study how localized circuit structure influences learning and barren plateau behavior in quantum neural architectures.','The Q-Capsule preprint provides the architecture-focused foundation. Evaluation should distinguish trainability, predictive performance and computational resources, using transparent simulation conditions and relevant baselines.','This contributes to experimental quantum and hybrid learning research. Improved trainability in a studied setting is not a claim of general quantum advantage or a deployed security capability.',['Localized circuits','Gradient & training behavior','Model evaluation']),
'Security and reliability of quantum AI':('Quantum and hybrid learning systems introduce architectural and execution assumptions that deserve explicit scrutiny. Their robustness cannot be inferred from either classical model results or the presence of quantum components.','Formulate reproducible tests for sensitivity to unreliable data, perturbations and execution conditions.','This remains a proposed direction. Controlled comparisons should separate circuit architecture, optimization, noise and adversarial assumptions; classical and hybrid baselines are necessary to interpret any future results.','The intended contribution is a rigorous basis for assessing quantum-learning reliability before operational use. No completed defense or security advantage is claimed for this direction.',['Threat assumptions','Controlled comparisons','Reliability evidence']),
'Multimodal robustness and agent interactions':('Vision–language models combine complementary evidence but can be vulnerable when an input is perturbed or modalities disagree. Interacting agents add another layer: one system’s outputs can become another system’s context or decision input.','Characterize robustness across modalities and investigate behavior across agent-to-agent interactions.','Published VLM robustness studies and emerging agent-interaction preprints form distinct evidence streams. The evaluation agenda connects input evidence, model response and interaction outcomes while keeping prototype results separate from deployment claims.','Relevant research settings include driving scenes, consumer AI and the VetClaw veterinary screening prototype. These studies do not establish validated autonomous driving, clinical screening performance or general-purpose agent reliability.',['Multimodal evidence','Models & agent interactions','Robustness assessment'])}
files=['ai-security-privacy-forensics','secure-sustainable-ai','quantum-ai-security','cyber-physical-systems','llms-multimodal-agentic-ai']
for name in files:
 f=p/(name+'.html');s=f.read_text()
 def enrich(m):
  a=m.group(0);title=re.search('<h3>(.*?)</h3>',a).group(1)
  if title not in D:return a
  desc,goal,approach,rel,flow=D[title]
  a=re.sub(r'(<h3>.*?</h3>)(?:<p>.*?</p>)?',lambda m:m[1]+'<p>'+e(desc)+'</p>',a,count=1)
  # Original cards place the intro in a wrapper; replace that paragraph too.
  a=re.sub(r'(<h3>.*?</h3><p>'+re.escape(e(desc))+r'</p>)(<p>.*?</p>)',r'\1',a,count=1)
  a=re.sub(r'<h4>Goals</h4><p>.*?</p>','<h4>Goals</h4><p>'+e(goal)+'</p>',a,count=1)
  a=re.sub(r'<h4>Intellectual Merit</h4><p>.*?</p>','<h4>Approach &amp; Intellectual Merit</h4><p>'+e(approach)+'</p>',a,count=1)
  a=re.sub(r'<h4>Applications</h4><p>.*?</p>','<h4>Relevance &amp; Applications</h4><p>'+e(rel)+'</p>',a,count=1)
  diagram='<div class="project-flow" aria-label="Conceptual research workflow">'+''.join('<span>'+e(t)+'</span>'+('<i aria-hidden="true">→</i>' if k<2 else '') for k,t in enumerate(flow))+'</div>'
  return a.replace('</article>',diagram+'</article>')
 s=re.sub(r'<article class="research-project">.*?</article>',enrich,s)
 if name=='ai-security-privacy-forensics':
  scope='''<section class="security-scope"><h2>Across Modalities, Platforms and Devices</h2><p>Our security, privacy and forensic research considers text, audio, images, video and sensing data across consumer platforms and connected devices, including large language models (LLMs) and vision–language models (VLMs). The central questions are what information can leak, how evidence can be manipulated, how model behavior changes, and what traces remain available for examination.</p><p>Published and preprint foundations include consumer writing and audio tools, image-edit provenance, wearable-data integrity, VLM robustness and AI application artifacts. Broader video and cross-device evaluation form part of the research agenda rather than a claim of completed coverage across every modality.</p><div class="scope-topics"><span>Text &amp; audio</span><span>Images &amp; video</span><span>Sensing data</span><span>LLMs &amp; VLMs</span><span>Consumer platforms &amp; devices</span></div></section>'''
  s=s.replace('<section class="research-pair section">',scope+'<section class="research-pair section">',1)
 f.write_text(s)
